/**

\addtogroup arrays

\tableofcontents

\section arrays-intro Introduction

Linx provides classes and services for handling one-dimensional (1D) and multi-dimensional (ND) arrays.
1D arrays are implemented by the `Sequence` class template, while ND arrays are implemented by the `Image` class template.
Both implement pixelwise operations and are compatible with global transforms such as filtering and Fourier transforms,
and both feature slicing capabilities for enabling regional operations and transforms.

Template parameters of these classes include the data type of the elements, the dimension or rank of the array,
and the underlying data container type.
The default data container type is generally a `Kokkos::View`, but other types can be used if needed.

```cpp
template <typename T, int N, typename TContainer = ImageContainer<T, N>>
class Image;

template <typename T, typename TContainer = SequenceContainer<T>>
class Sequence;
```

\section array-memory Memory management

The semantics of `Sequence` and `Image` are similar to those of `std::shared_ptr`:
memory is managed automatically, and can be shared between multiple objects without copying the underlying data.
Copy constructor and copy assignment operator perform shallow copy.
Memory is released when the last reference to the data is destroyed.

The memory layout (alignment, padding and element storage order) depends on the underlying machine architecture and the underlying data container type.
By default, `Kokkos::View` uses a machine-dependent layout, but this can be changed by specifying a different layout policy.
For example, `Raster` uses a contiguous, row-major ordered layout, which is compatible with most legacy libraries.

The container can be accessed through the `container()` method in order to interface with other Kokkos-based libraries.
It is also possible to obtain a pointer to the raw data using the `data()` method, which is useful for interoperability with other libraries.

\section array-datatypes Data types

Both `Sequence` and `Image` support numerical data types, including integers, floating-point numbers, and complex numbers, as well as strings.
Custom data types can also be used, with constraints inherited from the underlying data container type, such as `Kokkos::View`.
Typically, it is not possible by default to use non-POD types.

\section array-sequence-rank Sequence rank

For sequences, the term "rank" refers to the number of elements in the sequence.

There are two kinds of ranks for sequences:

- Static: the rank is known at compile time and is specified as template parameter `N`;
- Dynamic: the rank is not known at compile time and is specified at construction, with template parameter `N` set to `-1`.

Static-rank sequences ressemble standard arrays and are more efficient than dynamic-rank sequences,
while dynamic-rank sequences ressemble non-resizable standard vectors.

```cpp
auto static_rank_sequence = Linx::Sequence<int, 3>();
assert(static_rank_sequence.n == 3);
assert(static_rank_sequence.size() == 3);

auto dynamic_rank_sequence = Linx::Sequence<int, -1>(3);
assert(dynamic_rank_sequence.n == -1);
assert(dynamic_rank_sequence.size() == 3);
```

\section array-image-rank Image rank

For images, the term "rank" refers to the number of axes in the image.
The rank can be set statically or dynamically (with `N = -1`), similar to sequences,
but the number of elements is generally specified for each axis in the constructor.

```cpp
auto static_rank_image = Linx::Image<int, 3>(10, 20, 30);
assert(static_rank_image.n == 3);
assert(static_rank_image.rank() == 3);
assert(static_rank_image.shape() == Linx::Position({10, 20, 30}));
assert(static_rank_image.size() == 10 * 20 * 30);
assert(static_rank_image.extent(0) == 10);
assert(static_rank_image.extent(1) == 20);
assert(static_rank_image.extent(2) == 30);

auto dynamic_rank_image = Linx::Image<int, -1>(10, 20, 30);
assert(dynamic_rank_image.n == -1);
assert(dynamic_rank_image.rank() == 3);
assert(static_rank_image.size() == 10 * 20 * 30);
assert(static_rank_image.shape() == Linx::Position<-1>({10, 20, 30}));
assert(static_rank_image.extent(0) == 10);
assert(static_rank_image.extent(1) == 20);
assert(static_rank_image.extent(2) == 30);
```

The maximum rank of an image is determined by the underlying data container type, which is 8 for `Kokkos::View` and 7 for `Kokkos::DynRankView`.
However, many services offered by Kokkos are limited to views with up to 6 dimensions.
The Linx library itself supports arbitrary ranks.

0-dimensional images are supported.
As opposed to tensor libraries, they are not treated as scalars, but as empty containers.

```cpp
auto null_image = Linx::Image<int, 0>();
assert(null_image.n == 0);
assert(null_image.rank() == 0);
assert(null_image.size() == 0);
assert(null_image.empty());
assert(null_image.shape() == Linx::Position({}));
assert(null_image.data() == nullptr);
```

\section array-access Element access

Both `Sequence` and `Image` classes provide access to their elements through indexing operators.
For example, `sequence[i]` accesses the i-th element of a `Sequence`,
and `image(i, j)` accesses the element at position (i, j) in an `Image`.

`Sequence`s are always iterable, while `Image`s are iterable only if the underlying data is contiguous in memory.

\section array-slicing Slices and patches

Images and sequences feature slicing and patching capabilities that allow for efficient manipulation of subregions.
Slices are used to create subregion views as instances of `Image` or `Sequence`.
Therefore, slices have have a strided memory and their domains are necessarily regular, i.e., 1D intervals or ND axis-aligned boxes.
Slices are created with a syntax analogous to Python's slicing:

- `Slice()` means to select all elements along a dimension;
- `Slice(index)` selects a single index;
- `Slice(start, stop, step)` selects elements from index `start` to index `stop - 1`.

Such constructs can be combined to create multi-dimensional slices.

```cpp
// Keep x from 1 to 3, all y's, and z = 5
// The output slice is a 2D Image
auto slice = image[Linx::Slice(1, 4)()(5)];
```

By contrast, no assumptions are made on the memory layout of patches, therefore they support domains of any kind.
`Patch` is an extremely lightweight class that simply holds a domain and a pointer to the parent data.

```cpp
auto patch = Linx::where(image, weird_domain);
```

\section array-operations Operations

Images and sequences support a variety of operations, including arithmetic operations, element-wise functions, and reduction operations.
These operations can be performed on entire arrays or on specific subregions defined by slices or patches.

*/
